<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diaguita</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Diaguitas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Diaguita were a group of indigenous peoples speaking the Cacán language who lived in the valleys of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>. Skilled farmers, potters and metalworkers, they came partly under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> rule in the fifteenth century and fiercely resisted the Spanish in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calchaqui-wars">Calchaquí Wars</a> until the 1660s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diaguita.jpg" alt="Diaguita" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: B1mbo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Diaguita_people_in_Chile.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Western or Chilean Diaguitas lived mainly in the Transverse Valleys of the Norte Chico that cut through semi-arid mountains, and the Eastern or Argentine Diaguitas in what are now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rioja">La Rioja</a> and Catamarca and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salta">Salta</a>, San Juan and Tucumán. The name was first applied to these peoples and their archaeological cultures by Ricardo E. Latcham in the early 20th century. They were not a unified people: their speech seems to have varied from valley to valley, and they were divided into several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>.</p><p>Their culture is traced back to the El Molle complex of 300 to 700 CE, replaced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> by the Las Ánimas complex of 800 to 1000 CE, from which the archaeological Diaguita culture emerged around 1000 CE. Its classical period was marked by advanced irrigation and by pottery painted in black, white and red. Shells found high in the Andean valleys show trade between coast and interior.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> is generally held to have taken Diaguita lands through warfare that severely depopulated the Transverse Valleys; why it came, for minerals or for labour for the Chilean mines, is disputed. The Incas appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuraka">kurakas</a> and settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitma">mitmas</a> there, and the Diaguitas adopted pottery designs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a> and Inca methods of farming and metalworking. The eastern Diaguitas did not all accept Inca rule.</p><p>The first large Spanish encounter came in April 1536, when the expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro</a> reached the valley of Copiapó; there may then have been about 20,000 Diaguitas in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>. Under García Hurtado de Mendoza (1557–1561) rebel Chilean Diaguitas were decimated. On the eastern side the Spanish ringed the valleys with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-del-estero">Santiago del Estero</a>, Tucumán, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salta">Salta</a>, La Rioja and Jujuy, and founded Londres in their midst in 1607. The Calchaquí Diaguitas rose in 1630, nearly destroying La Rioja, and the last rebels fought until 1642–1643.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> the Diaguita languages may have been largely lost as the people mixed with Mapuche-speaking populations, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> communities in the southern Diaguita lands may go back centuries before the Spanish. Today 90,794 people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> identified as Diaguita or Diaguita-Cacano in the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and Chile, where 153,231 were counted in 2024, has recognised the Diaguitas as an Indigenous people since 2006.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diaguita” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1563 Philip II created the Governorate of Tucumán, Juríes and <b>Diaguitas</b>, separating it from Chile and placing it under the Audiencia of Charcas, with Santiago del Estero as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-tucuman">Governorate of Tucumán</a></li></ul></section>
